      <h2 id="introduction">Introduction to Personal Care Aerosols</h2>
      <p>Personal care aerosols are pressurised dispensing systems designed to deliver functional cosmetic products in a controlled spray form. Unlike creams or roll-ons, aerosols rely on a sealed package containing a liquid concentrate and a propellant, which together create atomisation upon actuation. This delivery method offers hygiene advantages — no direct skin contact with the container interior — ease of application, and consistent dosage coverage, which explains its global adoption across deodorant, body spray, antiperspirant, and fragrance categories.</p>
      <p>From an industrial standpoint, deodorant and body spray aerosols are not just cosmetic products — they are regulated pressure vessels with chemical, mechanical, and safety constraints that require cross-disciplinary understanding. The mechanics of <a href="/how-aerosol-cans-work/" target="_top">how aerosol cans work</a> — from propellant phase equilibrium to valve actuation dynamics — are the physical foundation of all personal care aerosol performance. Developing these products therefore requires expertise that spans formulation chemistry, aerosol engineering, regulatory compliance, and manufacturing process validation simultaneously. Industry production and sustainability data for the personal care aerosol sector are compiled by the <a href="https://www.bama.co.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">British Aerosol Manufacturers' Association (BAMA)</a> and the FEA.</p>

