      <h2 id="manufacturing-process">Manufacturing Process Overview (End-to-End)</h2>
      <p>Manufacturing personal care aerosols is a controlled industrial process governed by safety standards, quality management systems, and regulatory requirements that differ from those applicable to non-pressurised cosmetics. Aerosol manufacturing introduces specific risks related to pressure, flammability, and propellant gas handling, requiring purpose-built infrastructure including explosion-proof electrical systems, dedicated propellant storage and handling areas, and water bath pressure testing equipment. From bulk compounding to final packing, each step must be validated and documented to ensure consistency, compliance, and product safety at commercial volumes. The guide to <a href="/how-contract-aerosol-filling-works/" target="_top">how contract aerosol filling works</a> explains the commercial filling process step by step, including equipment, safety infrastructure, and quality checks.</p>

      <h3>Bulk Compounding</h3>
      <p>Bulk liquid preparation for personal care aerosols focuses on achieving homogeneity, stability, and compatibility — not merely mixing ingredients. Temperature control, agitation method, order of addition, and contamination prevention are critical, particularly for fragrance-containing systems where heat or incompatible surfaces can degrade volatile top-note components or cause phase instability. The concentrate must be stable at the fill temperature and compatible with all contact surfaces of the filling equipment, particularly stainless steel and polymer seals.</p>

      <h3>Aerosol Filling Line Configuration</h3>
      <p>Aerosol filling lines are purpose-built to handle liquids and propellants safely under controlled conditions. Line configuration for deodorant and body spray products depends on can size, propellant type (LPG requiring different explosion protection to compressed nitrogen), and target production volume. Filling methods — under-the-cup (UTC) or through-the-valve (TTV) — affect propellant loss factor, fill accuracy, and line speed. Automation level directly impacts consistency of crimp dimensions, fill weight, and headspace.</p>

