      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div style="margin-top: 20px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px;">
          <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 16px 20px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; font-size: 1.05rem;">What is the fundamental engineering difference between deodorant and body spray aerosols?</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 12px;">Deodorant aerosols are designed to deliver odour-control actives that reduce odour-causing bacteria at the application site, whereas body spray aerosols are primarily fragrance delivery vehicles. This functional difference drives distinct spray rate specifications, application zone targeting, usage frequency, and regulatory classification. Deodorants require precise, lower-volume targeted dose delivery; body sprays require wider spray coverage and higher fragrance load delivery across the body surface.</p>
          </details>
          <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 16px 20px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; font-size: 1.05rem;">What propellants are used in deodorant and body spray aerosols?</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 12px;">Deodorant and body spray aerosols most commonly use LPG (propane-butane blends) as the primary propellant, selected for consistent pressure profile and fine atomisation. DME is used where additional solvency is beneficial. Compressed nitrogen is used in sensitive formulations incompatible with hydrocarbon propellants. Propellant selection directly determines flammability classification, which governs labelling, transport, and storage requirements.</p>
          </details>
          <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 16px 20px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; font-size: 1.05rem;">Why is crimping integrity so critical in deodorant aerosol manufacturing?</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 12px;">Crimp integrity is the single most critical manufacturing quality attribute in aerosol production. An under-crimped valve allows propellant to leak, causing pressure loss and reduced product life. An over-crimped valve deforms the gasket elastomer, creating immediate or delayed leakage. At commercial scale, even a 0.1 mm systematic deviation in crimp diameter or depth across a batch creates significant product loss and potential hazardous pressure failures during distribution.</p>
          </details>
          <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 16px 20px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; font-size: 1.05rem;">What does IFRA compliance mean for fragrance in aerosol body sprays?</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 12px;">The International Fragrance Association (IFRA) publishes standards that restrict or prohibit certain fragrance ingredients based on skin exposure pathway and usage category. For body spray aerosols delivering fragrance directly to skin, IFRA standards specify concentration limits for sensitising, phototoxic, or restricted fragrance materials. Formulators must verify that the total fragrance compound in the aerosol concentrate complies with IFRA limits at the expected skin deposition concentration after atomisation.</p>
          </details>
          <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 16px 20px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; font-size: 1.05rem;">How do aerosol body sprays achieve wide coverage spray patterns?</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 12px;">Wide coverage spray patterns are achieved through actuator design — a larger-diameter swirl chamber with a wider discharge orifice converts pressurised liquid into a broad cone. The propellant vapour pressure determines exit velocity while actuator geometry determines droplet size and spray angle. Body spray actuators typically produce droplets in the 50–150 micron range to achieve fast skin deposition and minimal overspray drift in indoor environments.</p>
          </details>
          <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 16px 20px;">
            <summary style="font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; font-size: 1.05rem;">What are the key regulatory requirements for launching a deodorant aerosol in India?</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 12px;">In India, aerosol deodorants and body sprays are governed by the Cosmetics Rules under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act as cosmetic products. Key requirements include: INCI ingredient declaration, net content compliance under the Legal Metrology Act, hazard warning labels for flammable products, and manufacturing under a valid cosmetics manufacturing licence. Products making antiperspirant claims may be re-classified as drugs, triggering stricter clinical efficacy and GMP requirements.</p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

